5161d1c9 (1679 1793477734334 "guenchi" (("4gm4c2jr" . 3240) ("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . section) (title . "landing f400") (src . "working view: a writer's new files shown and its deleted ones hidden\n\nA writer's structural drafts, a new file and a deleted file, were held\nbeside its block drafts but outside its working view: the view was the\ncommitted blocks with the block drafts laid over, so outline, read and\ncontext with --working showed neither a new file nor the absence of a\ndeleted one, and only the file trees built from the view (export-code\n--working, edit-export, landing-freeze) did. A coder who added a file\ncould not ask context --for about its blocks before committing it.\n\nworking.sc: working-view-with-files is working-state's answer with the\nwriter's file drafts laid over the view as blocks (structural-layer). A\nnew file becomes the blocks its commit would make: the holder, named by\nthe draft's provisional id, with the fields file-new-intents gives it\n(kind file, mode text, path, language; no src), and the pieces named\n<provisional>/<K> in K order, split as the commit splits the file. The\nholder is placed where the commit's insert puts it, after the last\ntop-level block, and several new files follow in the order the drafts\nlisting gives them (provisional id, string<?), which is the order the\nlanding route commits them in. A deleted file's holder and the children\nits commit deletes are absent from the view. The view names a new block\nby a version a receipt can carry: the draft's version for the holder, a\nstructural-version over (new, piece name, sha256 of the bytes, cut) for\neach piece; reduce.sc keeps those versions and the hidden blocks'\nholders on the reduction (view-facts), block-hash answers the versions,\nand unknown-id answers a hidden block as (error unknown-id <id> (reason\ndeleted-in-working) (holder <holder>)), an additive clause; unknown-id\nkeeps its shape everywhere else. working-state itself is unchanged, so\nthe file trees built from it do not gain a new file twice. read\n--working takes no lock, so the listing of the writer's structural\ndrafts it now makes can name a key the same writer's discard unlinks\nbefore the read; active-structural drops such an entry instead of\nindexing it, which is what a read just after the discard finds, and\ngains the injection stage structural-list-before-read between the\nlisting and the reads (a stage not in ffi.sc's known-hold-stages is\nrefused at load, so the list gains it). The drafts listing\n(working-list) already listed without the lock and had the same race\nbefore this change; the same filter now covers it.\n\nread --working answers a new holder as a committed file holder reads\n(\"\") and a piece as its bytes; --working-info on either answers the\nprojection with the version the view names it by, the draft's cut and\nbased-on #f, a new block having no baseline. context --working takes the\nview with the files, so a piece of a new file can be the target and a\ncommitted block that uses a name the piece defines finds it among the\nname's definitions. outline gains --working: the outline of that view,\nending with (working <writer>); its signatures are still the committed\nstore's. A foreign eval --working projects the same view. A provisional\nid asked over any other view, without --working, in another writer's\nview and after the commit, is still answered provisional-id\nnot-committed. Commit is unchanged.\n\nTests: test/working-files-view.sc, new, 25 rows: the holder's place and\nthe order of two new files, reading the holder, the pieces and their\nprojection, context over a new piece and a committed importer of the new\nlibrary, another writer's view, a deleted file in outline, read and\ncontext, the foreign eval, and the view after the commit. Red first on\nthe base (15 rows), green on this tree; a mutant placing the holder\nfirst reds the two outline rows, a mutant dropping the hide rule reds\nWN-5's outline and context rows. context-users C1, C3 and C7 now read\nthe holder and a piece in the drafting writer's view. drafts-tools ST-R1\nholds a read --working of a committed block at the new stage while the\nwriter discards its one structural draft, and ST-R1b holds drafts the\nsame way: each red as working-unavailable with the stage alone, green\nwith the fix; both rows run the child with a hold longer than their\nwaits and assert that the hold did not expire. The one-row answer\nchanges take the tree's own routes: outline's describe entry in\nverb-registry-base.sexp, the describe answer listed in\nchange-stream-guard-expect.sexp, the two acknowledge-routes census\nentries moved to working-view-of, and liveness LV-6 leaving out the\nframe of a verb the guard's expect file lists, with a line per frame\nleft out. README: the commit paragraph that said only the file trees\nshow a new file is rewritten; read, context, outline, drafts and the\neval projection sentence say what the view now holds; the THEOURGIA_HOLD\nrow names structural-list-before-read.\n\nStage on an isolated copy of the tree (239 fixtures): only the known\npair red (f0-ondemand COST-1 over its budget, startup-ratio C1/C3);\nthree python fixtures run; row baseline hashes all the described\nversions; no process of the run left; alternation of the compiled\nproduct against the base median 1.0030 (p10 ratios 1.0167, 1.0030,\n0.9930), within 1%. Reviewed in three rounds; the second round added\nactive-structural's filter and the stage, the third the longer hold, the\ncheck that it did not expire, and ST-R1b.\n") (parent . root) (ord . 570))))
